`ca::syscall`: the kernel's own entry points, without libc. `ca::sys` calls these wherever the
target has a table, which is how ca reaches the kernel without the C library; user code should
prefer the typed `ca::sys` wrappers, since these check nothing beyond the argument count and kind.

- `syscall::invoke(number, args...)` makes any syscall with up to six arguments, each an integer,
  an enum or a pointer (`is_argument`), and returns the kernel's result or its errno as the error.
- With a table (`syscall::has_table`, Linux x86-64 today), `syscall::number::<name>` gives each
  number and `syscall::<name>(args...)` each call, with the kernel's own parameter names and
  arity: a call with the wrong number of arguments does not compile.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/process.hpp"
#include "ca/syscall.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	if constexpr (ca::syscall::has_table) {
		auto pid = ca::syscall::getpid();
		if (!pid || *pid != ca::sys::getpid())
			return 1;
		auto same = ca::syscall::invoke(ca::syscall::number::getpid);
		if (!same || *same != *pid)
			return 2;

		int ends[2]{-1, -1};
		if (!ca::syscall::pipe2(ends, 0))
			return 3;
		auto written = ca::syscall::write(ends[1], "ok", 2U);
		char got[2]{};
		auto read = ca::syscall::read(ends[0], got, sizeof got);
		if (!written || *written != 2 || !read || *read != 2 || got[0] != 'o')
			return 4;
		if (!ca::syscall::close(ends[0]) || !ca::syscall::close(ends[1]))
			return 5;

		auto closed = ca::syscall::close(-1);
		if (closed || closed.error() != ca::errc::bad_descriptor || ca::sys::last_error() != ca::errc::bad_descriptor)
			return 6;
	}
	return 0;
}
```

A failing call stores its errno in `sys::last_error()` as it returns: one thread-local store, no
call. On Linux x86-64, aarch64 and riscv64 the syscall is the trap instruction inlined at the call
site; on other Linux CPUs `invoke` goes through libc's `syscall(2)`, and on macOS, whose syscall
numbers are not a stable interface, it fails with `errc::not_supported`.

The table is generated by `tooling/gen_syscalls.py` (`make syscalls`) from the build headers of
the running kernel, so it contains exactly the syscalls that kernel implements; generated files
are never edited by hand.
